    "['no shutdown', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'delete disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\ndelete disable\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, Juniper devices enable interfaces by default, so in many cases the equivalent would simply be to omit any shutdown command. However, if explicitly needed, the command to enable an interface would be:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable\\ndelete interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: The first form `delete disable` is more concise and commonly used in Juniper configurations to enable an interface that was previously disabled. The second example shows the full path syntax for completeness.', 'trans_template': 'delete disable'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\ndelete disable\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, Juniper devices enable interfaces by default, so in many cases the equivalent would simply be to omit any shutdown command. However, if explicitly needed, the command to enable an interface would be:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable\\ndelete interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: The first form `delete disable` is more concise and commonly used in Juniper configurations to enable an interface that was previously disabled. The second example shows the full path syntax for completeness.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'delete interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\ndelete disable\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, Juniper devices enable interfaces by default, so in many cases the equivalent would simply be to omit any shutdown command. However, if explicitly needed, the command to enable an interface would be:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable\\ndelete interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: The first form `delete disable` is more concise and commonly used in Juniper configurations to enable an interface that was previously disabled. The second example shows the full path syntax for completeness.', 'trans_template': 'delete interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable'}]]": 1,
    "['no shutdown', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'delete disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent command for `no shutdown`:\\n\\n##\\ndelete disable\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if the interface is currently disabled and you want to enable it, you could also use:\\n##\\nset enable\\n##\\n\\nThe most direct translation is typically `delete disable` since Juniper interfaces are enabled by default and `disable` is the command that turns them off (analogous to Cisco's `shutdown`).\", 'trans_template': 'delete disable'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set enable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent command for `no shutdown`:\\n\\n##\\ndelete disable\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if the interface is currently disabled and you want to enable it, you could also use:\\n##\\nset enable\\n##\\n\\nThe most direct translation is typically `delete disable` since Juniper interfaces are enabled by default and `disable` is the command that turns them off (analogous to Cisco's `shutdown`).\", 'trans_template': 'set enable'}]]": 1,
